WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.600
In 2003, sequencing the first human genome took 13 years. It cost three billion dollars.

00:06.240 --> 00:11.120
Today, the same job takes a day costs about a thousand dollars. We've gotten extremely good

00:11.120 --> 00:16.800
at producing biological data. Billions of new sequences a year. The bottleneck isn't generating

00:16.800 --> 00:22.400
it anymore. It's making sense of it. The most fundamental question you can ask of a biological

00:22.400 --> 00:28.880
sequence is, have we seen something like this before? That's sequence alignment. Given two

00:28.880 --> 00:34.320
sequences, line up their characters to maximize matches, allowing for gaps where mutations have

00:34.320 --> 00:41.040
occurred. The score tells you how related they are. Do this against an entire database and you can

00:41.040 --> 00:46.640
identify a genes function, find evolutionary relatives or even predict a 3D structure.

00:47.520 --> 00:53.280
There are two main flavors of sequence alignment. Global alignment, using the Needleman-Wunsch

00:53.280 --> 00:58.720
algorithm, align sequences end to end. This is useful when you expect both sequences to be

00:58.720 --> 01:04.800
similar throughout. Local alignment, on the other hand, using the Smith-Waterman algorithm,

01:05.440 --> 01:10.720
finds the best matching sub-region. This is what you typically want for protein search,

01:11.280 --> 01:15.440
as a query might share just one functional domain with an otherwise unrelated protein.

01:16.240 --> 01:22.160
Both algorithms use dynamic programming. They fill an end-by-end table where every cell is the best

01:22.160 --> 01:28.880
score achievable so far. Each cell depends on its three neighbors, left, above and diagonal.

01:29.840 --> 01:35.440
The optimal global alignment is the score at the bottom right of the table. The best local alignment

01:35.440 --> 01:41.840
is the highest score in any cell. For protein specifically, not all amino acid swaps are equal.

01:41.840 --> 01:49.520
Substituting a hydrophobic residue for a similar one is far less destructive than swapping it for a charged one.

01:50.720 --> 02:00.160
So instead of a plus or minus one, we use BLOSUM62, a 24 by 24 substitution matrix derived from real evolutionary data.

02:00.160 --> 02:07.200
We also use affine gap penalties, a high cost to open a gap, but a lower cost to extend it.

02:08.160 --> 02:13.680
This accurately reflects biology, where single long insertions or deletions are much more common

02:13.680 --> 02:20.880
than multiple scattered ones. We can implement these algorithms in C++. Running one query against Swiss-Prot,

02:20.880 --> 02:26.880
a database of half a million proteins, the CPU manages about 0.3 giga cell updates per second.

02:26.880 --> 02:34.320
For TrEMBL, which is a database composed of 200 million sequences, that's days per query.

02:35.280 --> 02:42.240
The standard tool, BLAST, gets around this by using heuristics, but it sacrifices the optimality guarantee.

02:43.520 --> 02:51.680
We still want exact answers, just a lot faster. GPUs are built exactly for this kind of parallel workload.

02:51.680 --> 02:58.320
A modern NVIDIA GPU is made up of thousands of CUDA cores, grouped into streaming multiprocessors,

02:58.960 --> 03:06.240
each executing groups of 32 threads called warps. All threads within a warp execute the same instruction

03:06.240 --> 03:13.280
simultaneously, but on different data. The function executed by each thread is called a kernel.

03:14.080 --> 03:18.240
When a kernel is launched, these threads are organized into a grid of thread blocks.

03:18.240 --> 03:25.280
At the hardware level, each block is assigned a streaming multiprocessor and executes independently.

03:27.120 --> 03:33.920
GPUs have several distinct memory spaces, each with different capacity, latency and scope characteristics.

03:35.680 --> 03:38.240
Registers are the fastest and are local to each thread.

03:38.240 --> 03:45.040
Shared memory is small, has very low latency, and is accessible by all threads in a block.

03:46.640 --> 03:50.720
Global memory is abundant, visible to everyone, but very slow.

03:51.840 --> 03:55.200
Constant memory is also visible by all threads, but is read-only.

03:56.480 --> 04:01.440
The simplest way to parallelize these algorithms is by assigning one thread per alignment.

04:02.240 --> 04:06.400
Each thread takes one database sequence and fills its own dynamic programming table.

04:07.200 --> 04:13.200
If we run this on a graphics card with 2300 CUDA cores, throughput doubles.

04:14.640 --> 04:17.840
That's it. That's probably lower than you might have expected.

04:18.480 --> 04:20.000
There are three main reasons for this.

04:20.880 --> 04:24.240
Number one, memory access is not coalesced.

04:24.240 --> 04:29.600
Adjacent threads in a warp grab residues from completely different regions of memory,

04:30.320 --> 04:33.920
so a single warp load fragments into 32 separate transactions.

04:35.840 --> 04:39.680
Number two, the full DP matrix is in global memory.

04:40.320 --> 04:47.280
The third reason, BLOSUM62 is in constant memory, which is great when threads read the same address,

04:47.280 --> 04:48.720
but here, they don't.

04:49.760 --> 04:51.520
Every thread looks at a different residue.

04:52.160 --> 04:55.120
Four improvements can drastically increase performance.

04:55.120 --> 04:59.600
Instead of storing the database as a flat array, where each sequence is packed side by side,

05:00.480 --> 05:04.400
we can interleave the kth residue of 32 sequences side by side,

05:05.280 --> 05:08.720
so a warp's load becomes one coalesced transaction.

05:09.600 --> 05:14.880
Each cell only depends on the previous row, so we don't need to store the whole matrix in memory.

05:15.760 --> 05:16.880
We only need two rows.

05:16.880 --> 05:23.680
The memory used per sequence now drops from big O of M multiplied by N to big O of N.

05:25.040 --> 05:28.000
Instead of storing our substitution matrix in constant memory,

05:28.640 --> 05:31.280
we can load it into shared memory at the start of the kernel.

05:32.080 --> 05:36.080
After that point, every lookup hits ultra-fast on-chip cache.

05:37.120 --> 05:41.360
Almost every Swiss-Prot alignment fits comfortably in a signed 16-bit range.

05:41.360 --> 05:49.120
We can use 16-bit integers and flag all the overflows on the fly, and re-compute these rare offenders with 32-bit integers instead.

05:49.120 --> 05:54.960
Once we combine these, along with a few other techniques, our kernel is nearly 10 times faster

05:54.960 --> 06:00.240
in the naive implementation. Around 9 giga-cell updates per second for Smith-Waterman.

06:01.040 --> 06:03.120
That's 30 times faster than the CPU.

06:03.680 --> 06:10.480
But on a uniform-length synthetic database, the exact same kernel hits 15.5 giga-cell updates per second.

06:11.360 --> 06:13.360
On real Swiss-Prot, only 9.

06:14.400 --> 06:15.680
That's a 40% drop.

06:15.680 --> 06:20.000
The culprit here is that not all sequences have the same length.

06:21.440 --> 06:26.000
Even after sorting, sequences within the 32-wide warp aren't identical.

06:26.960 --> 06:32.160
The longer sequence dictates how long every other lane has to wait, so some threads are masked out.

06:32.720 --> 06:34.880
For long sequences, we need a different strategy.

06:35.760 --> 06:36.960
Look at that recurrence again.

06:37.840 --> 06:40.480
Every cell on the same antidiagonal is independent,

06:41.600 --> 06:44.400
so we can compute the entire antidiagonal in parallel.

06:45.680 --> 06:47.280
This is called the wavefront strategy.

06:48.000 --> 06:53.600
We can break the matrix into tiles and dispatch one thread block per tile along the macro diagonals

06:54.240 --> 06:55.680
and synchronize between waves.

06:57.280 --> 07:02.480
Within each tile, we can fill the sub-matrix using the same wavefront strategy again,

07:02.480 --> 07:03.520
but on a thread level.

07:04.480 --> 07:09.440
Now, an entire grid of blocks can cooperate in one alignment, instead of just a single thread.

07:10.240 --> 07:12.720
If we plot both kernels against sequence length,

07:12.720 --> 07:14.800
inter wins for short sequences.

07:16.080 --> 07:19.840
There's a lot of short, independent alignments that can keep the GPU busy.

07:20.560 --> 07:26.000
However, intra wins for long sequences, where the wavefront is wide enough to occupy the SMs.

07:26.880 --> 07:29.280
They cross at around 1200 residues.

07:30.400 --> 07:32.080
Each is optimal in a different regime.

07:32.640 --> 07:35.280
To achieve the best performance, we need to use both.

07:35.280 --> 07:38.000
We pick a threshold, tau.

07:38.560 --> 07:40.480
Below it, route to inter.

07:41.040 --> 07:42.800
Above it, route to intra.

07:43.680 --> 07:45.840
The question is, what is tau?

07:47.280 --> 07:52.000
If we sweep it on Swiss-Prot, the optimum is around 8192.

07:53.120 --> 07:56.880
Around 0.1% of Swiss-Prot is longer than 8k residues.

07:57.760 --> 08:00.160
That tiny fraction is dragging the performance down.

08:00.960 --> 08:03.280
Reroute them, and the bottleneck vanishes.

08:04.000 --> 08:04.640
The summary?

08:05.520 --> 08:11.360
On Swiss-Prot, with a 464 residue query, the throughput and giga cell updates per second

08:12.240 --> 08:17.200
is 0.3 for the CPU baseline, 0.65 for the naive GPU,

08:17.200 --> 08:24.000
9 for the optimized inter alone, and 13.4 for the tuned hybrid.

08:24.960 --> 08:29.040
Averaged out across queries from 64 to 496 residues.

08:29.600 --> 08:32.080
That's a 38 times speedup over the CPU.

08:33.200 --> 08:36.560
And a 5.7 times speedup over single-threaded Parasail.

08:37.600 --> 08:42.560
A state-of-the-art, single-instruction, multiple-data CPU library for Needleman-Wunsch.

08:43.760 --> 08:45.920
All scores are verified against Parasail.

08:46.800 --> 08:50.640
What this project shows is that raw parallelism alone isn't enough.

08:51.440 --> 08:55.520
You need the right hardware, the right memory layout, and the right strategy.

08:55.520 --> 08:57.440
Thanks for watching.

08:57.440 --> 08:58.960
I hope you enjoyed it.

